            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"MATCH\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus identifies the symbolic meaning of 'Jew' as an authentic, inward participant in God's covenant (one whose heart is transformed by the Spirit), while also recognizing its frequent literal ethnic and religious sense. The consensus points primarily to Romans 2:28-29 and even notes the etymological connection between Judah and 'praise.'\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'Jew' in covenant contexts as 'one in the covenant' or an 'inward' Jew, explicitly linking the definition to the etymology of Judah ('praise') and Romans 2:29 ('whose praise is not of men, but of God'). It also notes that the term is frequently used in its plain ethnic and religious sense.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"An inward, authentic member of God's covenant people, as distinct from mere outward ethnic descent.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"One in the covenant ('inwardly'), defined by the name's meaning of 'praise' toward God, rather than merely outward ethnic/religious dress.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"None. Both the consensus and the book differentiate between the plain ethnic referent and the inward, covenantal referent.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both the book and the consensus identify the same twofold usage: a literal ethnic/religious designation and a spiritual 'inward' covenantal designation. Furthermore, both explicitly highlight the connection between Romans 2:29 and the Genesis etymology of Judah meaning 'praise.'\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"None. The agreement on both the primary spiritual meaning, the literal meaning, and the precise etymological wordplay is complete.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
